    {
      "kind": "statement",
      "eyebrow": "01 · The thesis",
      "title": "The technology is not the story. The cost line is.",
      "body": "Discretionary spending is a contest for attention and convenience. Agents and AR collapse the distance between intent and purchase, while robotics and autonomy attack the two biggest cost lines: labour and fulfilment. Brands keep pricing power only where taste, status or service cannot be delegated to software."
    },
    {
      "kind": "points",
      "eyebrow": "02 · Sector economics",
      "title": "What this sector actually sells",
      "body": "Revenue is cyclical and promotion-driven; cost is dominated by labour, rent, inventory and marketing. Small changes in markdown or fulfilment cost swing operating margin.",
      "bullets": [
        "Retail earns on gross margin and inventory turns, not headline sales.",
        "Store economics depend on four-wall sales per square foot and payroll.",
        "Autos earn on mix, financing and after-sales; capex is model-cycle driven.",
        "Marketplaces earn take rate and advertising, with logistics as the moat.",
        "Hospitality and travel earn on occupancy and revenue per available room."
      ]
    },
